---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import { site } from '../lib/data.mjs';
import Icon from '../components/Icon.astro';

const { code } = await site();
const counter = `import { component, view } from 'effectweb';

const Counter = component<{}, { count: number }>(
  { init: () => ({ count: 0 }) },
  view((model, patch) => (
    <button onClick={() => patch({ count: model.count + 1 })}>
      Count: {model.count}
    </button>
  )),
);`;
---

<Site
  title="Web interfaces with Effect"
  description="EffectWeb is a client-side UI framework for immutable Effect models, familiar JSX, and direct DOM rendering."
  section="home"
>
  <main id="main" class="landing">
    <section class="hero">
      <div class="hero-copy">
        <h1>Web interfaces,<br /><em>built with Effect.</em></h1>
        <p class="hero-description">
          Build interactive browser apps with TypeScript, JSX and Effect. Views are functions of
          immutable data, work is an Effect owned by the component or controller that started it,
          and services come from an Effect context. There is no second state model to translate
          into.
        </p>
        <div class="hero-actions">
          <a class="button primary" href="/docs/getting-started/"
            >Get started <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a
          >
          <a class="button text-button" href="/docs/why-effectweb/"
            >Why EffectWeb <Icon name="arrow-right" /></a
          >
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="hero-example">
        <div class="example-caption"><span class="file-label">counter.tsx</span></div>
        <Fragment set:html={code(counter)} />
        <p class="example-footnote">
          The click handler patches the state. The view runs again with the new value.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
    <section class="principles" aria-label="Main ideas">
      <article>
        <h2>Effect all the way to the screen</h2>
        <p>
          Work is an Effect run under a Scope, services come from a Context, and the status of a
          request is an AsyncResult. Disposing a component interrupts its work the way Effect does.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/concepts/">Core concepts <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} /></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Mistakes are caught before they run</h2>
        <p>
          The lint rejects impure renders, async handlers and unkeyed rows, with the fix in the
          message. Development builds freeze published data, so a mutation throws where it happens.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/compiler/">Compiler and lint <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} /></a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Rows keep their identity</h2>
        <p>
          Reorder a list without moving focus, drafts or running work to another item. Keys follow
          the data; state that must survive removal belongs in the parent.
        </p>
        <a href="/docs/lists/">Lists <Icon name="arrow-right" size={14} /></a>
      </article>
    </section>
    <p class="landing-limit">
      Best suited to interactive apps such as editors, dashboards and chat clients. APIs are still
      evolving; server rendering and hydration are not provided. <a href="/docs/why-effectweb/"
        >What it does and does not do <Icon name="arrow-right" size={13} /></a
      >
    </p>
  </main>
</Site>
